\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Local Gaussian data and marginal restriction}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Fix a finite vertex set #{V}. For every subset #{T\subseteq V}, let
#{\mathcal G(T)} be the set of centered real Gaussian probability laws
on #{\mathbb R^T} with positive-definite covariance. Put
#{\mathcal G(\varnothing)=\{1\}}, the unit mass on the zero-dimensional
space. For #{S\subseteq T}, let
#{\pi_{S,T}:\mathbb R^T\to\mathbb R^S} be coordinate projection.
Its adjoint, for the \vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002},
extends a function on #{S} by zero. Marginal restriction is
##{\operatorname{res}_{S,T}\mu=(\pi_{S,T})_*\mu.}
If #{\mu=N(0,G)}, the restricted covariance operator is
#{\pi_{S,T}G\pi_{S,T}^{\dagger}}. Identity and composition of
projections make #{\mathcal G} a [presheaf](tt-002Q) on the poset of
all subsets of #{V}.}

\p{A cover of #{T} is a finite family #{(T_i)} with
#{\bigcup_iT_i=T}. A family #{\mu_i\in\mathcal G(T_i)} is
\newvocab{compatible local Gaussian data} if its two marginal laws on
each #{T_i\cap T_j} agree. A global extension is a law
#{\mu\in\mathcal G(T)} with these restrictions.}

\p{The domain contains all subsets and all such positive Gaussian laws.
It is not restricted to the Markov laws of one fixed graph: marginalizing
vertices can create new precision entries. For a law with precision
#{Q=G^{-1}} and both #{S} and #{S^c=T\setminus S} are nonempty,
write #{\iota_S} and #{\iota_{S^c}} for the counting inclusions of those
coordinate subspaces. With the blocks #{A,K,D} of \ref{fdip-005M},
the [Schur formula](fdip-000D) gives the precision of the
#{S}-marginal as
##{(\pi_{S,T}G\pi_{S,T}^{\dagger})^{-1}=A-KD^{-1}K^{\dagger}.}
This is generally different from the compression #{A=\iota_S^{\dagger}Q\iota_S}.
Empty blocks follow the zero-dimensional convention. The principal
submatrices of these operators are given in \ref{fdip-00LU}.}

\p{The [two-singleton example](fdip-006A) shows why overlap
compatibility alone does not ensure uniqueness.}
